{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head af599ecf55ca3adf0c9d806821c1bd5354010c4c on request/perf-worker-stdout, against request f916fe5c (#22345) and promise bf4d8bb3, primary ce570174 at cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go and companion 1a32016c at cmd/gitseq-perf/worker_process_test.go. Two paths, one commit over main d6981d91; git merge-tree --write-tree main af599ecf is clean at tree b4230c79.\n\nArchitecture. The affected layer is 7, the CLI and tooling layer, in the gitseq-perf performance harness alone. runWorkerDiagnostic at cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go:533-546 now binds separate stdout and stderr buffers and decodes stdout only; runWorker at 502 remains a pass-through, so no caller signature moves. The worker sample schema, the contract timeouts, the argv the parent builds, and every kernel, fold, host and projection interface are untouched. docs/reference/architecture.md does not mention this command, the worker, or any stdout/stderr protocol, and docs/reference/performance.md mentions the worker only for timeout lengths and the trusted-projection diagnostic. No documented contract changes and no architecture page amendment is owed. The head preserves the layer 7 contract.\n\nSecurity. No execution boundary moves: the worker binary is still an explicitly selected path passed to exec.CommandContext with no shell, and the argument vector is byte-identical to main. The substantive gain is that untrusted runtime diagnostics no longer cross into the JSON trust boundary; stderr is now structurally incapable of reaching the decoder rather than merely unlikely to. DisallowUnknownFields at 543 and validateWorkerResult at 547 still refuse, and both survive mutation of the surrounding capture. Nonzero exit still wraps *exec.ExitError through %w, and the timeout at 516 is unchanged and independently proven live: replacing context.WithTimeout with context.WithCancel makes TestWorkerProcessRetainsTimeout fail after 30 seconds. On bounding, stderr is untrusted and both buffers are unbounded, but this is not a regression: CombinedOutput buffered and interpolated exactly the same bytes, so the worst-case error size is identical to main, and the request explicitly excludes a new output-cap mechanism. The test helper writes its fixture to a temporary path containing a space and passes it as argv rather than through a shell, so the space is a real probe and there is no injection surface; all script payloads are fixed literals sitting as printf operands, not format strings. Authority: request f916fe5c is live and addressed to codex, promise bf4d8bb3 rests on it, the primary rests on the promise and is the newest artifact on it, both artifacts stand at the same head, and there is no describes_superseded_world; the universal stale flag traces to eadab5d4, the sibling batch-test request, which is ordinary staleness. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSimplification. The change is one buffer pair and two error strings, with the successful stderr discarded by omission rather than by any suppression mechanism. No process wrapper, no diagnostic store, no generalisation to other call sites. The test file is a single table plus one timeout case and a four-line helper, and its exec sleep 30 avoids a sleeping child holding the pipes open, a real subtlety correctly handled and explained. Nothing to cut. Two observations that weaken no condition and do not warrant changes here: mutation shows no test pins stdout retention or the stdout:/stderr: labels in the error paths, though the request's stated condition, preserve useful stderr on process and decoding failures, has a firm red proof across four assertions; and the indirect parser at output() (main.go:1046) feeding strconv.ParseInt (586) and resolveCommit (1010) is the same defect class but is explicitly bounded out by this request and belongs in its own child request. No other production CombinedOutput site feeds a decoder.\n\nWhat I ran. In a detached read-only worktree at the exact head, under Go 1.26.7: full package test ok 3.685s exit 0; go vet ./cmd/gitseq-perf/ exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; git diff --check main..af599ecf exit 0; go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/gitseq-perf/ ok 5.084s exit 0; git merge-tree --write-tree main af599ecf exit 0. In a separate git archive copy I reverted the decode to CombinedOutput and reproduced the claimed failure exactly, \"decode worker result: invalid character 'w' looking for beginning of value: worker diagnostic\", failing the success, unknown-field and wrong-axes controls together. Three further mutants: dropping stderr from the error paths fails four assertions; dropping the timeout fails the timeout test; dropping stdout or the stream labels is not caught. Every attached evidence claim checks out: validation.json's pinned source_sha256 for both files match the head's blobs exactly, the full-package 3.593s and control 0.394s transcripts match my own reruns, and the depth-10 probe records the same correctness digest 4b37c6a66983aa under ordinary and GODEBUG=gctrace=1 runs; the probe is not in the head, and the diff is exactly the two filed paths. One nuance: the recorded original-control transcript prints an earlier draft's failure text (successful worker result = ...) rather than the head's test wording, so that control ran against a slightly earlier test draft; my rerun against the head's exact test file reproduces the same failure, and validation.json pins the head's real hashes. I did not verify main CI and make no performance claim. Note for the merge: the one live predecessor at cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go is 6b52728d, authored by claude, within this verdict's reviewed paths, so the exact-path retirement is covered by this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ce570174937504b4cac7b9c6e5ba3b7e7b4c294e","binding":"assigned","head":"af599ecf55ca3adf0c9d806821c1bd5354010c4c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a7cf7cdc9a2f781726e